// ADVERSARIAL PROBE (infer-wrong lens, round 4, 2026-08-06): a DIFFERENT
// blind spot from the already-known `attack_bare_fn_param_alias_defeats_
// hotparam.kry` (which uses `let g = f; return g()`, a fresh `let` alias).
// This probe targets `build_local_closure_caps_block` (checker.rs ~2219),
// which builds the flat `local_caps` map consulted at every closure CALL
// site. Its `match stmt` handles `Stmt::Let`, `Stmt::If`, `Stmt::For`/
// `Stmt::While`, `Stmt::TryCatch`, and a bare `{ }` block -- there is no
// arm for `Stmt::Assign` at all, so a REASSIGNMENT of an existing `let mut`
// fn-typed local (as opposed to a fresh `let` binding) never updates that
// local's tracked provenance in the flat map. Two independent, cooperating
// consequences:
//   (1) Seed A (`compute_hot_params`, checker.rs ~1445) only marks a
//       parameter hot when `walk_calls_block` finds a call whose CALLEE
//       IDENTIFIER NAME textually equals the parameter's own name. Here the
//       only call in the body is `state()` -- "state" never equals the
//       parameter name "cb" -- so `cb` is never marked hot, exactly as with
//       the alias case, but reached via `Stmt::Assign` instead of a fresh
//       `let`.
//   (2) Because `Stmt::Assign` is invisible to `build_local_closure_caps_
//       block`, `local_caps["state"]` is frozen at whatever the ORIGINAL
//       `let mut state = || "safe default"` resolved to (`Known(empty)`)
//       even after `state = cb` rebinds it to the caller-supplied closure.
//       The real per-call checker (`collect_caps_expr`'s `FnCall` arm ->
//       `resolve_direct_invoke_caps` -> `resolve_closure_caps`'s
//       `Identifier` arm) consults that SAME stale map at the `state()`
//       call site.
// Net effect: `apply_reassign`'s own inferred capability set comes out
// EMPTY (not `all`, not `DependsOnParam(cb)` -- just silently empty), and
// since it has no hot-param entry, NO caller of `apply_reassign(...)` is
// ever charged for whatever closure it passes either. The privileged
// closure's authority disappears completely instead of surfacing at either
// end -- exactly the failure mode invariant 22 exists to rule out.
//
// Positive control: `attack_reassign_local_defeats_hotparam_control.kry`
// calls the parameter DIRECTLY (`cb()`, no local/no reassignment) and is
// correctly rejected -- confirming the reassignment indirection, not
// something generic about a bare fn-typed parameter, is what defeats the
// guard.

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

fn apply_reassign(cb: fn() -> str) -> str {
    let mut state: fn() -> str = || "safe default, no privilege"
    state = cb
    return state()
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/attack_reassign_local_defeats_hotparam_secret.txt")
    deny!(fs:read) {
        let leaked = apply_reassign(reader)
        println("REASSIGN-LOCAL LEAK: " + leaked)
        sleep(100)
    }
}
